# The hero's crawl: 11 poses, used in BOTH directions.
#
# Direction is deliberately absent from these names, and that is a finding
# rather than caution. `crawl_one_cycle_two_labels.py` showed CAVES indexes
# one cycle forwards as HERO_CRAWL_LEFT_PHASE_n and backwards as
# HERO_CRAWL_RIGHT_PHASE_(15-n) -- six pieces claimed both ways, the two
# phase numbers summing to exactly 15 every time.
#
# That left open whether one labeling was simply wrong. The anchor arithmetic
# settles it: every LEFT routine moves HERO_X NEGATIVELY (-1..-4) and every
# RIGHT routine moves it POSITIVELY (+1..+4). Both directions are real
# animations, and they draw the same eleven pictures. So the art is
# direction-neutral and a LEFT or RIGHT in its name would be false half the
# time.
#
# POSE, not F: a piece is phase n crawling left and phase 15-n crawling
# right, so no member has a single frame number.
#
# The index is the LEFT cycle's own phase order, rebased from 2..12 to 0..10,
# where phases 11 and 12 are the RIGHT-only pieces mapped through
# left = 15 - right. Read off the machine's numbering, not assigned. It
# independently reproduces the rotation this bank uses elsewhere -- ascending
# offset with the LOWEST offset last, the same rule as
# ../actor-sprite-sheet/walk_left_upper.map and walk_right_upper.map:
#
#   0x47DC 0x485C 0x48D8 0x4954 0x49B0 0x4A04 0x4A6C 0x4ACC 0x4B4C 0x4BC4
#   then 0x4754
#
# Geometry is one class: 13.0..15.5 tall, 13.0..19.0 wide, 5..9 leaves, all
# drawn at [HERO_X] only, none behind a guard.
CV_47DC HERO_CRAWL_POSE_00
CV_485C HERO_CRAWL_POSE_01
CV_48D8 HERO_CRAWL_POSE_02
CV_4954 HERO_CRAWL_POSE_03
CV_49B0 HERO_CRAWL_POSE_04
CV_4A04 HERO_CRAWL_POSE_05
CV_4A6C HERO_CRAWL_POSE_06
CV_4ACC HERO_CRAWL_POSE_07
CV_4B4C HERO_CRAWL_POSE_08
CV_4BC4 HERO_CRAWL_POSE_09
CV_4754 HERO_CRAWL_POSE_10
